,"sa"],["pg",""],["hs","ns"],["hs","ss"],["at","South must lose 1 spade trick "],["pg",""],["ls","ns"],["ls","ss"],["hs","nc"],["hs","sc"],["at","and 2 club tricks. "],["pg",""],["ls","nc"],["ls","sc"],["hs","nh"],["hs","sh"],["at","The contract will depend on not losing a trump trick."],["pg",""],["md","0,c3,,h9"],["ls","nh"],["nt","South should start by leading a heart "],["pg",""],["ls","sh"],["pc","h"],["hc","hak"],["at","to the @HA or @HK in dummy. "],["pg",""],["lc","hak"],["pc","h"],["pc","hk"],["pc","h"],["pg",""],["hs","eh"],["hs","wh"],["at","If both opponents follow small South must hope that hearts are breaking 2-2.\n\n"],["pg",""],["ls","wh"],["up","1"],["md","0,h9,,c3"],["at","If East follows with the @HJ or @HQ, "],["pg",""],["pc","h"],["pg",""],["md","0,c3,,hj"],["hc","ha"],["hs","eh"],["at","South must decide how to proceed. Cashing dummy's other heart honour will work when East started with a doubleton @HQJ."],["pg",""],["lc","ha"],["ls","Eh"],["pc","ha"],["pc","H"],["pc","h"],["pc","h"],["pg",""],["up","4"],["md","0,hj,,c3"],["hs","eh"],["nt","\nSouth can also play East for a singleton honour in hearts. "],["pg",""],["ls","eh"],["hs","nd"],["hs","sd"],["at","In this case, he must cross back to his hand "],["pg",""],["ls","nd"],["ls","sd"],["pc","D"],["pc","d"],["pc","dk"],["pc","d"],["pg",""],["hs","nh"],["hs","sh"],["at","and finesse the @H10."],["pg",""],["ls","nh"],["ls","sh"],["pc","h"],["pc","h"],["pc","h"],["pc","c"],["pg",""],["up","12"],["hs","nh"],["hs","sh"],["nt","\nThe second round heart finesse is in fact the superior play. The mathematics that prove this assertion are quite complex. The ^*BPrinciple of Restricted Choice^*N is the name that bridge players use to denote this type of situation."],["pg",""],["hc","hqj"],["ls","nh"],["ls","sh"],["nt","Restricted choice applies when you have to decide between two possible defensive holdings involving cards of equal rank (the @HQ and @HJ in this hand). Under these circumstances the odds favour playing the defender who has played one honour (the @HQ) not to have the other one (the @HJ)."],["pg",""],["md","0,c3,,hj"],["nt","\nAnother way to explain this principle is that if East has both the @HQ and @HJ he has a choice between which heart honour to play. There is some inference that East doesn't have the @HJ because he might have played it if he had it."],["pg",""],["md","0,hj,,c3"],["lc","hqj"],["hs","eh"],["nt","\nIf East was dealt a singleton @HQ (or @HJ) he has to follow suit with that card on the first round of the suit. "],["pg",""],["ls","eh"],["pc","h"],["pc","h"],["pc","hk"],["pc","h"],["pg",""],["hs","nh"],["hs","sh"],["at","His choice is restricted. Declarer should play East to have played the @HQ because he had no choice and finesse on the second round."],["",""]]}}